<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//W3C//DTD HTML 4.01 Transitional//EN">
<html>
<head>
<title>water cycle</title>
<meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html; charset=iso-8859-1">
</head>

<body bgcolor="#333333" text="#CCCCCC" link="#666666" vlink="#999999" alink="#FFFFFF">
<blockquote>
  <p align="center"><font color="#000000" size="7">Deforestation and the Water Cycle</font></p>
  <p align="center"><img src="watercycle.gif" width="504" height="378"> </p>
  <p align="center"><a href="www.rainforests.net">source, www.rainforests.net</a></p>
  <p align="left">It is widely believed that the most important nutrient for life 
    is water. In the 21st not every child has access to clean and healthy water, 
    cities in the developing world are turning to the private sector to bring 
    water to their people, and we continue to interfere with one of the most nutrient 
    cycles on Earth. Humanities interference with this crucial cycle has often 
    proved to be fatal to people and animals from all over the world. Either accidentally 
    or as a byproduct of industry, deforestation, or other types of resource use. 
    Various examples of fatal alterations include, the Colorado River which no 
    longer reaches the ocean and is a mere salty trickle when it even reaches 
    Mexico, The Cuyahouga River in Cleveland, Ohio which actually caught fire 
    from all the pollution in the late 70's. Even still Americans are warned not 
    to eat more that 3 Lake Erie Walleye per year.</p>
  <p align="center"><img src="ddbac021.jpg" width="461" height="304"></p>
  <p align="center"><a href="www.mongabay.htm">www.mongabay.htm</a></p>
  <p align="left">Above, Deforestation in Panama on the edge of a National Park.</p>
  <p align="left">&nbsp;</p>
  <p align="left">The picture below shows a landslide after a rainstorm that was 
    caused by strip deforestation that weakened the topsoil. With the torrential 
    downpour the loose soil was not restrained and killed approximately 500. (BBC 
    news)</p>
</blockquote>
<p align="center"><img src="_1120741_tecla_landslide300.jpg" width="315" height="180"></p>
<p align="center"><img src="watercycleportrait.jpg" width="777" height="538"></p>
<p>Water is either evaporated or transpired at all elevations but in order for 
  there to be precipitation the atmosphere needs to become unstable. This arises 
  when a block of air is lifted. Either it runs into a high pressure ridge or 
  a mountain range. The air is lifted and cools as it rises. If as it rises high 
  enough and the temperature falls enough, so that the humidity exceeds the dew 
  point, it will rain. At this point the rain eithers throughfalls the trees and 
  enters a river basin or is transpired by the leaves back to the atmosphere. 
  If the rain enters the waterbasin and there are no longer any trees, there is 
  increased erosion and muddying of the water, decreasing the amount of sunlight 
  many water plants need to survive thereby, killing them. In fact, there are 
  many coral reef ecosystems around the globe that are threatened due to the muddying 
  of coastal waters directly resulting from deforestation.</p>
<p align="center"><img src="db.gif" width="240" height="170"></p>
<p>Now should the water be not through fall to the Earth, and instead land on 
  leaves or simply be absorbed in the roots of the tree, then the water through 
  evapotranspiration. Below is a diagram illustrating the process of photosynthesis 
  and how that results in the transpiration of water. Typically in tropical rainforests 
  this results in a 2-3.5 degree Celsius drop in temperature.</p>
<p align="center"> <img src="evapotranspire.gif" width="720" height="415"></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The loss of forests immediately increases the habitat temperature, as well 
  as, the soil temperature. In some places this coupled with excessive erosion 
  can result in desertification. Below is an example of how much the temperature 
  of South America would warm up should the Amazon continue to be deforested.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p align="center"> <img src="rosebush_Pristine_sm.jpg" width="254" height="300"></p>
<p>Beautiful flowers like this transpire water through photosynthesis to cool 
  themselves and the air around them. Its kind of like perspiration.To conceptualize 
  think about when you get out of the shower and are instantly cold as the water 
  evaporates off your body. It takes a lot of energy to evaporate water and the 
  coldness you feel is the water usurping your energy to evaporate.</p>
<p>In fact, the amount of the sun's energy that falls on the Amazon Rainforest, 
  which is used for evaporation and transpiration, is 49%. That represents a dramatic 
  cooling effect on the biome. </p>
<p align="center"><img src="heattransfer.gif" width="378" height="522"></p>
<p align="center">Measuring Evapotranspiration is difficult and inexact at best, 
  but for a summary of methods <a href="http://web.mit.edu/12.000/www/m2006/teams/r6/evapotranspiration.html">click 
  here</a></p>
<p align="center">&nbsp;</p>
<p align="left">Below is how the water cycle is influenced byt the absence of 
  forests. B,C,D,and E represent the how deforestation increases erosion while 
  A shows represents the increase in temperature and other effects stemming from 
  the loss of evapotranspiration. This diagram shows how each of these effects 
  dominos through the water cycle causing vast changes.</p>
<p align="center"><img src="deforestationwatercycle.gif" width="720" height="524"></p>
<p align="center">&nbsp;</p>
<p align="left">The Water Cycle is dramatically impacted by deforestation, as 
  the water that falls to earth runs to the oceans and freshwater reserves. The 
  impact is, in contrast with deforestation's impact on the Carbon Cycle, local. 
  Therefore, these effects of deforestation are felt by the local people and can 
  not be represented by graphs such as the level of carbon in the atmosphere. 
  Rather it is by the sediment load of streams, the loss of biodiversity, and 
  the barren landscapes that indicate the effects of deforestation in the Water 
  Cycle. </p>
</body>
</html>

